# encoder_hotspot_profile_2026-08-02 — provenance
# Companion to encoder_hotspot_profile_2026-08-02.md and its four TSVs.
# This is the FIRST profile of the ENCODER in this repo; every prior profiling
# artifact under benchmarks/ is decode-side.

date                 2026-08-02
box                  Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GiB, macOS 26.5.2 (Darwin 25.5.0, arm64)
repo commit          0953fa758cf2612d962fb0c8429c01b9abb4251b   (worktree zenav1-aom--eprof, detached at main)
libaom oracle        upstream/ submodule 03087864cf4bea6abb0d28f95cf7843511413d8f  (v3.14.1)
rustc                1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)
C compiler           Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.1.1.101)
python               3.14.6
build flags          --release, NO -C target-cpu=native (runtime SIMD dispatch, what users get)
threading            single-threaded on both arms (the port has no threading; the oracle
                     libaom is built CONFIG_MULTITHREAD=0)
nice                 every measured run under `nice -n 19`

## BOX WAS NOT IDLE — read this before reading any absolute millisecond

A `zenmetrics` mac-worker fleet (11 processes) held ~312 % CPU of the box's
1200 % for the whole session; whole-box load average 25-29. It was another
agent's live work and was NOT interfered with. Consequences, all of which are
handled rather than hoped about:

* Every port/libaom-c comparison is INTERLEAVED (control) or back-to-back
  (breadth), so drift lands on both arms.
* The control band was re-measured under exactly this load and is reported
  first (2.88 % spread port / 5.06 % C over 9 independent invocations).
* Sampling SHARES are shares within one process and are largely insensitive to
  a background load that leaves ~9 cores free; the absolute ms/encode figures
  they are multiplied by come from the same runs.

## Encoder arms (identical to benchmarks/xbench, deliberately)

port        benchmarks/xbench/drv-aom          `EncodeCell::port_encode_with(&bootstrap, default knobs)`
                                                timed region = the port's whole frame encode; the
                                                UNTIMED C sequence-header bootstrap is structural
                                                (the port never authors a sequence header).
libaom-c    benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c timed region = aom_codec_encode + drain + flush + drain;
                                                enc_init/destroy/img_alloc excluded.
                                                MEASURED untimed fraction: 200 reps, sum(NS) 9.556 s vs
                                                9.57 s wall = 0.15 % — so the sampled window is
                                                99.85 % timed encode and needs no correction.

## Profile cell

1024x1024 photo, cq-level 44, cpu-used 6, ALLINTRA (usage 2), 8-bit 4:2:0,
single tile, single thread. Source: $HOME/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv, produced by
`python3 scripts/xbench.py prep` (Lanczos3 square:1024 downscale of
codec-corpus clic2025/final-test/8426ed22...png), byte-identical to the file the
2026-08-01 cross-encoder study used.
Both arms emit the SAME 4472-byte stream at this cell (byte-identical, re-verified
here at 256x256, 1024x1024 and 2048x2048 and at cpu-used 3,4,5,6,7,8).

## Tooling — and what it can and cannot tell you

profiler             /usr/bin/sample (macOS built-in), 1 ms interval, 60 s window,
                     started 5 s after launch so the .yuv read, the C bootstrap and the
                     warm-up encode are all outside it.
  * It is a WALL-CLOCK SAMPLER, not an instruction counter. The decode-side
    profiles in this directory used callgrind (Ir, deterministic, load-independent);
    valgrind is not available on Apple Silicon, so that method is not on the table
    here. Read shares as shares of elapsed time, with the sampling error of
    ~45 000 samples.
  * It does NOT expand inlined frames. Self cost is charged to the outermost
    NON-inlined function, so e.g. `cnn_predict`'s self includes its inlined
    `conv_valid`. The headline finding is independently cross-checked by a
    direct per-call microbenchmark (eprof_cnn_bench) and by an exact call count
    (eprof_alloc), which do not depend on the profiler at all.
  * The port arm is a `CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=2` build (+ dsymutil) so the
    symbolication resolves. Debug info does not change codegen: the debug build
    produced a BYTE-IDENTICAL .obu and ran at 514.3 ms vs the plain release
    build's 506.2 ms — inside the 2.88 % control spread.
  * libaom.a carries no -g, so its `static` functions symbolicate to the nearest
    preceding exported symbol. Global functions (which is nearly all of the top
    of the profile) are exact.

rollup               scripts/eprof_rollup.py — self(node) = count - sum(children),
                     summed over every call site (exact, and correct under the
                     recursion in rd_pick_partition_real); inclusive counted at
                     outermost occurrences only. Symbols classified to stages by
                     name; the unmatched residual is printed, not hidden
                     (0.23 % port / 3.28 % libaom-c).
alignment            scripts/eprof_align.py — each side's self-share x ITS OWN
                     measured ms/encode, which is the only form in which the two
                     are comparable.
caller attribution   scripts/eprof_callers.py — charges a leaf class (allocator /
                     memset / memcpy) to the nearest non-matching ancestor.
alloc census         crates/aom-bench/examples/eprof_alloc.rs — counting GlobalAlloc,
                     exact call counts and byte totals for one encode, plus exact-size
                     watch counters that give the CNN cascade's call count directly.
CNN per-call bench   crates/aom-bench/examples/eprof_cnn_bench.rs — port vs libaom `_c`
                     vs libaom dispatched (NEON), same window, outputs compared before
                     any timing is printed.
control band         scripts/eprof_control.sh — 9 independent process invocations per
                     arm, interleaved, each 2 warm-up + 7 timed encodes, own median.
breadth              scripts/eprof_breadth.sh — the same two arms over the size,
                     cpu-used and quantizer axes, plus the exact CNN call count per cell.

## Exact commands

  git worktree add --detach ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--eprof main
  git -C ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--eprof submodule update --init

  nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p zenav1-aom-sys-ref          # the C oracle
  nice -n 19 cc -O2 -o benchmarks/xbench/target/drv_libaom \
      benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c -Iupstream -Iupstream/build \
      -Lupstream/build -laom -lm -lc++
  (cd benchmarks/xbench && nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  (cd benchmarks/xbench && CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$HOME/tmp/eprof-dbg-target \
      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=2 nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p drv-aom)
  dsymutil ~/tmp/eprof-dbg-target/release/drv-aom
  nice -n 19 cargo build --release -j 4 -p zenav1-aom-bench \
      --example eprof_alloc --example eprof_cnn_bench

  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_control.sh 9 ~/tmp/eprof_control.tsv
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh port-dbg  150 60 ~/tmp/eprof_port.sample.txt    5
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_sample.sh libaom-c 1500 60 ~/tmp/eprof_libaomc.sample.txt 5
  python3 scripts/eprof_rollup.py ~/tmp/eprof_port.sample.txt    --arm port     --rules rust --tsv ~/tmp/eprof_port.tsv
  python3 scripts/eprof_rollup.py ~/tmp/eprof_libaomc.sample.txt --arm libaom-c --rules c    --tsv ~/tmp/eprof_c.tsv
  python3 scripts/eprof_align.py --port ~/tmp/eprof_port.tsv --port-ms 511.689 \
                                 --c    ~/tmp/eprof_c.tsv    --c-ms    48.171
  python3 scripts/eprof_callers.py ~/tmp/eprof_port.sample.txt --leaf '<allocator class>'
  ./target/release/examples/eprof_alloc     1024 1024 44 6 ~/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv
  ./target/release/examples/eprof_cnn_bench 1024 1024        ~/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv 2000
  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_breadth.sh ~/tmp/eprof_breadth.tsv

## Denominators used in the aligned table

port      511.689 ms/encode   (median of the 150 timed reps taken DURING the profile run)
libaom-c   48.171 ms/encode   (median of the 1500 timed reps taken DURING the profile run)
ratio during profiling 10.62x; ratio in the clean interleaved control 10.72x.
The .md quotes the control-band ratio for the headline and the during-profile
medians for the aligned table, so each number is paired with the run it came from.

## Raw artifacts NOT committed (regenerate with the commands above)

  ~/tmp/eprof_port.sample.txt      1.7 MB  `sample` call graph, port
  ~/tmp/eprof_libaomc.sample.txt   ~1 MB   `sample` call graph, libaom-c
  ~/tmp/eprof-dbg-target/                  the debug-info build + its .dSYM (19 MB)

## Known gaps in this measurement, stated so nothing above is over-read

* ONE image, one content class (photographic). No screen content, no synthetic.
* 8-bit 4:2:0 only; no 10-bit, no 4:4:4, no monochrome.
* cpu-used 0, 1 and 2 were NOT profiled or breadth-measured (each is minutes per
  encode at 1 MP and the sweep budget went to the axes the qualifying mode sits on).
  cpu-used 9 REFUSES on this cell at this commit — see the .md.
* No instruction-count profile (no valgrind on Apple Silicon), so IPC and cache
  behaviour are not separated from instruction count the way the decode-side
  gate3 profiles separate them.
* No multi-threaded measurement. Neither arm is threaded here.
